WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:08.370
He found the most important fold in biology flat on his back, with a sinus infection

2
00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:09.470
and a sheet of paper.

3
00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:15.390
No lab, no model kit just a chain he refused to force closed after a whole number of

4
00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.190
residues, the way everyone else had assumed it must.

5
00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:23.290
Folding the paper at roughly the right bond angles, he let the geometry decide.

6
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:29.530
What fell out was untidy on purpose: a right-handed helix that came around every 3

7
00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:36.150
.6 residues, rising 5.4 angstroms per turn, with each carbonyl oxygen reaching up

8
00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:41.530
the axis to clasp the amide hydrogen four residues along—a regular ladder of

9
00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:44.270
hydrogen bonds running parallel to the helix axis.

10
00:00:45.183 --> 00:00:48.503
He had read the structure off the bonding, the way he read everything.

11
00:00:49.163 --> 00:00:54.803
He called it the alpha helix, and he could not let it go: "I was so pleased with

12
00:00:54.803 --> 00:00:58.883
this structure that I stayed up until late in the night, pondering about it," he

13
00:00:58.883 --> 00:00:59.883
recalled.

14
00:01:00.033 --> 00:01:02.073
Experiment confirmed it only later.

15
00:01:02.433 --> 00:01:03.873
The geometry had been enough.
